          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 117",
          "text": "1. **Installed-base replacement opportunity — Analyst Interpretation.** E2/E7 support recurring replacement needs across proprietary components and multiple applications; qualification friction can make substitution difficult, helping revenue durability and debt-service support. Counterevidence is the absence of generally committed aftermarket volumes and sensitivity to fleet activity, customer finances and procurement. The view would weaken with sustained loss of installed-product positions, qualification problems or deterioration in delivered aftermarket orders; verified long-term demand coverage would strengthen it.",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 119",
          "text": "2. **Demonstrated operating profitability and cash generation — Analyst Interpretation.** E3/E4 show resilient operating margins and improving annual cash conversion, providing resources for interest and reinvestment before discretionary deployment. Counterevidence is fixed-price cost exposure, working-capital consumption, unvalidated add-back recurrence and excluded acquisition/distribution uses. The view would weaken with persistent margin compression coupled with poor operating-cash conversion, or strengthen with a supported cash-earnings bridge and sustained conversion through weaker demand.",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 121",
          "text": "3. **Historical financing execution and reporting base — Analyst Interpretation.** E6/E8 document completed refinancings/par repayments and audited annual reporting, supporting a traceable historical analysis and evidence of access then. Counterevidence includes secured-priority changes, acquisition-control exclusions and the unresolved maturity schedule. Reconciled maturities, operative documents and completed control integration would strengthen the view; failed financing execution or adverse reporting/control developments would weaken it.",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 123",
          "text": "**Weaknesses**",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 125",
          "text": "1. **Cash retention subordinated to discretionary deployment — Analyst Interpretation; key risk severity: Material.** E1/E5 document acquisition spending, cash payouts and a debt-funded dividend declaration; transferring resources to owners while debt service remains interrupts organic deleveraging and reduces creditor flexibility. Counterevidence is genuine operating cash generation and no program repurchases in FY2024/FY2023 (CP-1A TX05), neither of which neutralizes actual distributions. Sustained retention and debt repayment without replacement borrowing would improve the view; further leveraged payouts would weaken it.",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 127",
          "text": "2. **Leveraged funding structure makes operating setbacks consequential — Analyst Interpretation; key risk severity: Material.** E5/E6 and the checked leverage/coverage figures show a meaningful debt burden relative to non-GAAP earnings; weaker earnings or cash retention would increase refinancing dependence. Counterevidence is improved historical ordinary-EBITDA/net-interest coverage and completed financing transactions. A reconciled funding timetable and retained-cash deleveraging would improve the view; lower serviceability, higher funding cost or restricted access would worsen it. Missing maturity/legal information is a restriction, not proof of a near-term wall or breach.",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 129",
          "text": "3. **Common sector and execution dependencies despite product breadth — Analyst Interpretation; key risk severity: Material.** E2/E3/E7/E8 link aerospace use/production, government procurement, qualified suppliers and repeated acquisition integration to orders, margins and cash. Correlated disruption can overwhelm individual product diversification and magnify leverage pressure. Counterevidence includes defense/commercial exposure, multiple components and management's historical aftermarket-stability characterization, which remains unquantified. More granular platform/customer exposure and demonstrated acquired-unit returns could improve the view; qualification failures, delayed deliveries, adverse procurement events or weak acquired-unit cash returns would weaken it.",
